HOPE Coding Ecosystem Integration Requirements Draft

Purpose

This document records the requirements for adding ECC and future coding ecosystems to HOPE.

This is a requirements draft only. It is not coding instructions, an implementation plan, or a patch plan.

HOPE must remain universal, so ECC must not be added as a one-off hardcoded integration. The same architecture should later support Cline, Ruflo, Hermes, OpenCode, Qwen Code, Gemini CLI, Aider, local coding agents, and remote coding agents.

---

1. Main architectural decision

HOPE must upgrade the existing Agent Manager instead of creating a separate top-level Coding Agent Manager.

HOPE should also add a reusable internal service called something like:

Coding Ecosystem Integration Service

This service is not a new independent manager. It translates external coding ecosystems into HOPE-managed resources and connects them to the existing managers.

ECC should become a managed "coding_agent_ecosystem" resource.

The architecture should be:

- Repository Manager acquires and verifies the ecosystem.
- Coding Ecosystem Integration Service understands its components.
- Skill Manager manages its skills and workflows.
- Capability Manager evaluates whether its capabilities are usable.
- Agent Manager registers and coordinates its agents.
- Sandbox Manager controls execution.
- API Manager provides models and providers.
- Security Manager scans its code and configuration.
- Conversation Manager provides direct conversations and task interaction.

The system must continue using HOPE’s universal management bus, shared lifecycle contract, permissions, readiness system, and dashboard model.

---

2. Definition of a coding ecosystem

A coding ecosystem is larger than one AI model or one agent.

It may contain:

- Specialized agents
- Skills
- Commands
- Rules
- Hooks
- Memory workflows
- Context files
- MCP configuration
- Tool definitions
- Security scanners
- Runtime scripts
- Dependency requirements
- Model requirements
- Repository workflows
- Testing workflows
- Review workflows
- Installation and update logic

ECC should therefore be treated as a bundle of related resources.

Its components must remain connected to the parent ECC ecosystem while also being registered inside their appropriate HOPE managers.

ECC must not be copied into one folder and executed as an uncontrolled package.

---

3. Repository Manager ownership

Repository Manager owns the source and lifecycle of ECC.

It must handle:

- GitHub repository discovery
- GitLab and local repository discovery
- Repository acquisition
- Release selection
- Commit and tag pinning
- Source hashing
- License detection
- Dependency discovery
- Security analysis
- Repository caching
- Updates
- Version comparison
- Diffs
- Disposable worktrees
- Rollback
- Import preparation
- Handoff to the correct managers

Repository Manager must not execute agents just because it downloaded a repository.

Its responsibility is:

Acquire → verify → inspect → prepare → hand off

When ECC is imported, Repository Manager should produce an import package containing:

- Repository source
- Selected release or commit
- Source hash
- License information
- Detected components
- Dependencies
- Runtime requirements
- Permissions
- Security findings
- Compatibility information
- Proposed manager assignments
- Update and rollback information

Repository Manager must preserve the original source identity and must never replace it with an unofficial or unverified copy without clearly reporting that change.

---

4. Coding Ecosystem Integration Service

The Coding Ecosystem Integration Service is the translator between an external ecosystem and HOPE.

It must:

- Read ecosystem manifests
- Detect agents, skills, commands, rules, hooks, tools, and runtimes
- Normalize different external formats
- Preserve relationships between components
- Create HOPE resource records
- Route skills to Skill Manager
- Route agents to Agent Manager
- Route capability requirements to Capability Manager
- Request sandbox profiles
- Request API/model bindings
- Send components for security review
- Coordinate readiness checks
- Provide ecosystem-level status
- Support enable, disable, update, rollback, and removal
- Prevent duplicate installation
- Track ownership of imported files and resources

It must not own:

- Repository storage
- API credentials
- Skill execution
- Shell execution
- Security approval
- Final permission decisions

Those responsibilities remain with the existing managers.

The service must be generic. It should not contain ECC-only logic that prevents other ecosystems from being added later.

