12. API Manager and model coordination

ECC must not independently manage HOPE’s API credentials or provider registry.

API Manager must own:

- Providers
- Endpoints
- Credentials
- Models
- Quotas
- Rate limits
- Health checks
- Routing
- Fallbacks
- Protocol drivers
- Token budgets
- Privacy policies

ECC agents should request model capabilities rather than hardcoded provider names.

A request may specify:

- Coding support
- Tool calling
- Required context size
- Structured output
- Reasoning quality
- Privacy requirement
- Cost limit
- Latency limit
- Local-only requirement

API Manager then selects the appropriate connected provider.

Possible routing examples:

- Planning: reasoning-capable model
- Implementation: coding-capable model
- Large repository analysis: long-context model
- Security review: security-focused model
- Simple formatting: economical model
- Offline work: local model

API Manager must provide clear failure states when:

- Provider credentials are missing
- Model is unavailable
- Quota is exhausted
- Endpoint is unhealthy
- Required protocol is unsupported
- Model does not support tools
- Context window is too small

